commentBody:
@Steve L

That crossed my mind too, but it solved pretty easily as a themeless, so I hope it doesn't cause too much pain. 

When I was very young, I was very into MLB, but it's been several decades since I've paid attention. I didn't know any of the themers as a reference to baseball, but they were gettable—JACKFRUIT and TATERTOT without any crossings.

@Steve L

It's fairly easily gettable even if you know nothing about baseball.

I'd never heard of JACK for a homer.

@Steve L

As usual, I find the idea of a "hard" puzzle that can be solved in a mean time of five minutes "hard" to accept.

I will nevertheless confirm that I solved it 10% slower than my Monday average.  That probably demostrates that it was relatively hard for me.

@Steve L

I came here expecting to see all kinds of complaints that it was too hard for a Monday (INURN, HUA, and LINER, I'm looking at you!), even though I somehow solved it 42 seconds under my Monday average time according to xwordstats.

So it was one of those puzzles that felt harder than it solved, if that makes sense...

@Selective Walrus
I had heard this line long ago but never knew its origin.  Whether Groucho actually said it seems open to question, based on my Google search.  That said, I understand the speculation that it could well have been he, having regard to a classic line that Dr. Spaulding came out with in Animal Crackers:

“One morning, I shot an elephant in my pyjamas.  How he got into my pyjamas, I dunno.”

@Kevin

FWIW, recent article about armadillos moving north:

<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/14/us/armadillo-ohio.html" target="_blank">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/14/us/armadillo-ohio.html</a>

@SBK in TO
I thought of chaos but I would never put it in without checking the crosses.  The old school pre-computer pen in the daily newspaper habits of mine dictate that I couldn't bring myself to fill in "making passes" like so many folks here speak of doing.  Just foreign to me.  (Whew!  Gladl, finally, to get that off my chest.)
To each their own!

@Bill in Yokohama 

Wrong. 

The lead-off hitter is the first batter of any half inning.

@Brooks

Agreed.

Lead-off homers and lead-off hitters are two different things.

@Mike

The author traveled to the game  by train on a round tripper.

If it wasn't well-researched, he would have been knocked out of the park.

@Al in Pittsburgh

Luckily, he was able to grab a couple of sliders for lunch in the club car on the way.

He rounded his snack off with--peanuts and Cracker Jacks!!

@Muzzy

"Please remove this general topic from all puzzles intended for a general audience because I don't like this general topic."

Funny stuff!

@Jamie

Heh, now I see it 😃

(It doesn't though, not in Polish. Our "ch" is your "h" - your "ch" is our "cz")

@Matt

I'd have to disagree with your last sentence.  I can't recall a single incidence of a snow game in the postseason.  MLB's website has a page about snow games, and they're all in the early spring.  There's one picture of batting practice for the 1997 Series where it's snowing, but the game itself was apparently played snow-free (albeit in 38-degree temperatures (approx. 3 Celsius).  But that's about it.

With more southern teams and domed stadiums, snow games are less likely (although not impossible).  That and the fact that the Colorado Rockies aren't going to make the playoffs any time soon.

@Steve L

Agree to disagree.

There is an profound dimming of the world's esthetic light every year when the formal hibernation of daily, continent-wide ball-field apotheosis commences.

@Steve L,

Re your last sentence:

Truer words were never spoken.

@Gareth Baird
Nothing is too hard for we Scots!  Mind what you say!

* I have the Ancestry report to prove it...

@Gareth Baird
No True Scotsman would have any trouble with this puzzle.

( had to do it)

@Billy

🇵🇱🤜🏾🤛🏾🇬🇧

Spot on!

@Billy

And I was actually waiting for a Brit to balance the Americanism of this puzzle with a good old "innit" 😃

Thank you mate!

@N.E. Body

SCAN can go either way:

<a href="https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/scan" target="_blank">https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/scan</a>

@Sam Corbin

I just looked it up and apparently they're called contronyms.

It's funny that we do that.  I wonder if that happens with other languages.

@Mean Old Lady
I have occasionally heard a commentator say "He really jacked that one" after a long hit.  Not sure of its origin.

Go Broors!

@jma,

I usually hear it as a verb, as “He really JACKed that one!” . 

I only hear it as a noun when it’s pluralized with  two homers in a row, and announcers are contractually obligated to say they are “back-to-back JACKs!” 😄

@Nancy
Same sadness here except the DODGERS  for me. I became a Mets fan for a bit, but it wasn't the same.
Now that we live in the Boston area I have lots of people to root AGAINST the Yankees with (in fact I'd better!) but not the same either.

@J

I wonder if there's ever been a puzzle when someone somewhere didn't say "_____ is not a word and has no place in a crossword puzzle".  

You might as well just say, "I didn't know the word, so it must not be a word."

@Pax Ahimsa Gethen
Watching baseball since the 80s. I've heard BOMB, JACK, TATER, and BLAST all used for that glorious thwack followed by the smooth arc of a ball leaving the stadium.
(A sad dearth of those for my beloved Jays this year. 《sniffle》 )
